// @test: run
// @level: L2
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, project, mount, cross-file
// @timeout: 60000
// @project: two_file_project
// @expected-stdout: 7
// @description: A library module beside a main runs.
//
//   This is the first shape any program takes once it outgrows one file,
//   and until this spec existed the suite could not express it. Measured
//   across 6949 specs: not one `verum.toml`, and every one of 8560 `mount`
//   statements points at the standard library — no spec imports a sibling
//   spec. The defect below therefore did not slip past the suite; the suite
//   had no way to see it.
//
//   What it hid: `verum check` accepted the project and `verum run` and
//   `verum build` both failed with
//
//       error<E402>: module `probe.lib` not found
//
//   The two commands disagreed about which modules exist. `check` discovers
//   project files by walking from the input, while the loader that `run` and
//   `build` share required a `mod.vr` at the project root and returned early
//   without it — so the ordinary layout, `verum.toml` with `src/main.vr`
//   beside `src/lib.vr`, loaded no sibling modules at all. A project could
//   be green in an editor, in CI and under `check`, and still not start.
//
//   The `@project:` directive names a sibling DIRECTORY to compile instead
//   of this file: the `.vr` carries the directives and the expectation, the
//   directory carries the program. Its negative pole is
//   `two_file_project_rejects_unknown_module.vr`, which keeps a fix from
//   passing by making `run` accept everything.
//
//   Task: T0826.

fn main() {
    print("placeholder — see @project: two_file_project");
}
